a mock:\n\n```python\ndef test_islurp_closes_file(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    p = tmp_path / \"f.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"data\\n\")\n    real_open = open\n    opened = []\n    def tracking_open(file, mode='r', *args, **kwargs):\n        fh = real_open(file, mode, *args, **kwargs)\n        opened.append(fh)\n        return fh\n    monkeypatch.setattr(\"builtins.open\", tracking_open)\n    assert list(islurp(str(p))) == [\"data\\n\"]\n    assert len(opened) == 1\n    assert opened[0].closed\n```\n\nThat works and verifies the close postcondition. monkeypatch.setattr(\"builtins.open\", tracking_open) \u2014 but the module uses `open` which resolves to builtins.open at call time, so patching builtins.open works.\n\nWait, but the module might do `from io import open`? The shown code uses plain `open`. In pytutils/files.py, there might be `from .compat import ...`. The extracted code shows `import os, sys, functools` and uses `open`. Patching builtins.open should work.\n\nHmm, but careful: monkeypatch.setattr(\"builtins.open\", ...) \u2014 pytest's monkeypatch supports string target \"builtins.open\". Yes.\n\nTest 2: string 'LINEMODE':\n```python\ndef test_islurp_string_linemode(tmp_path):\n    p = tmp_path / \"s.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"a\\nb\\n\")\n    assert list(islurp(str(p), iter_by='LINEMODE')) == [\"a\\n\", \"b\\n\"]\n```\nCovers lines 23-24.\n\nTest 3: chunked:\n```python\ndef test_islurp_chunks(tmp_path):\n    p = tmp_path / \"c.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"abcdefgh\")\n    assert list(islurp(str(p), iter_by=3)) == [\"abc\", \"def\", \"gh\"]\n```\nCovers the functools.partial branch of line 37 and EOF break.\n\nTest 4: stdin:\n```python\ndef test_islurp_stdin(monkeypatch):\n    fake_stdin = io.StringIO(\"from stdin\\nline2\\n\")\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sys, \"stdin\", fake_stdin)\n    assert list(islurp(\"-\")) == [\"from stdin\\n\", \"line2\\n\"]\n    assert not fake_stdin.closed  # stdin must not be closed\n```\nCovers lines 28-29, 39-43, and line 45 false b