d there; it's in else branch). Wait \u2014 look at the code: `fh_next = fh.readline if ...` is inside the else block (line 37, indented under else). For stdin, fh_next = sys.stdin.readline implicitly? Line 40 `buf = fh_next()` \u2014 for stdin case, fh_next is... wait, fh_next is only assigned in the else branch! If filename == '-', fh_next is undefined \u2192 NameError at line 40!\n\nHmm wait, let me re-read the extracted code:\n\n```\n        26:     fh = None\n        27:     try:\n        28:         if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n        29:             fh = sys.stdin\n                    else:\n        31:             if expanduser:\n        32:                 filename = os.path.expanduser(filename)\n        33:             if expandvars:\n        34:                 filename = os.path.expandvars(filename)\n            \n        36:             fh = open(filename, mode)\n        37:             fh_next = fh.readline if iter_by == LINEMODE else functools.partial(fh.read, iter_by)\n            \n        39:         while True:\n        40:             buf = fh_next()\n```\n\nHmm, line 37 is inside the else (indented at the same level as line 36). So for stdin, fh_next would be undefined \u2192 NameError. Unless the actual code has `fh_next = fh.readline ...` outside the if/else. Let me check the real pytutils source:\n\nFrom memory of pytutils/files.py (sarugaku/pytutils):\n\n```python\ndef islurp(filename, mode='r', iter_by=LINEMODE, allow_stdin=True, expanduser=True, expandvars=True):\n    \"\"\"\n    Read [expanded] `filename` and yield each (line | chunk).\n\n    :param str filename: File path\n    :param str mode: Use this mode to open `filename`, ala `r` for text (default), `rb` for binary, etc.\n    :param int iter_by: Iterate by this many bytes at a time. Default is by line.\n    :param bool allow_stdin: If Truthy and filename is `-`, read from `sys.stdin`.\n    :param bool expanduser: If Truthy, expand `~` in `filename`\n    :param bool expandvars: If Truthy, expand env vars in `filename`\n    \"\"\"\n    if iter_by == 'LINEMODE':\n        iter_by = LINEMODE\n\n    fh = None\n    try:\n        if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n            fh = sys.stdin\n        else:\n            if expanduser:\n                filename = os.path.expanduser(filename)\n            if expandvars:\n                filename = os.path.expandvars(filename)\n\n            fh = open(filename, mode)\n\n        fh_next = fh.readline if iter_by == LINEMODE else functools.partial(fh.read, iter_by)\n\n        while True:\n            buf = fh_next()\n            if buf == '':  # EOF\n                break\n            yield buf\n    finally:\n        if fh and fh != sys.stdin:\n            fh.close()\n```\n\nI believe in the real source, `fh_next = ...` is OUTSIDE the else (dedented), because otherwise the stdin path would crash. The extracted snippet's indentation is ambiguous due to the line-number annotations. The snippet shows line 37 with leading spaces \"            fh_next = ...\" \u2014 12 spaces, same as line 36 \"            fh = open(filename, mode)\" which is inside else (8 spaces for else body? hmm).\n\nLet me count indentation in the snippet:\n- `    if iter_by == 'LINEMODE':` \u2014 4 spaces (function body).\n- `        iter_by = LINEMODE` \u2014 8.\n- `    fh = None` \u2014 4.\n- `    try:` \u2014 4.\n- `        if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:` \u2014 8.\n- `            fh = sys.stdin` \u2014 12.\n- `        else:` \u2014 shown as `                    else:`? The snippet shows \"                    else:\" \u2014 hmm, that's odd, it shows with more indentation but that's likely formatting artifact. Actually the snippet:\n\n```\n        28:         if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n        29:             fh = sys.stdin\n                    else:\n        31:             if expanduser:\n```\n\nThe \"else:\" line has no line number (it's not a numbered line since `else:` lines often aren't counted... actually they are). The formatting is mangled. Line 31 `            if expanduser:` \u2014 12 spaces \u2192 inside else. Line 36 `            fh = open(filename, mode)` \u2014 12 spaces \u2192 inside else. Line 37 `            fh_next = ...` \u2014 12 spaces \u2192 inside else?? Then stdin path \u2192 NameError.\n\nHmm wait, but line numbers 30 and 35, 38 are missing from the annotation \u2014 those are probably blank lines or the `else:` line. The annotation shows lines 23,24,26,27,28,29,31,32,33,34,36,37,39,40,41,42,43,45,46. Missing: 25, 30, 35, 38, 44. Line 30 would be `else:` and 44 `finally:`. So the else: is line 30, finally: is line 44.\n\nSo per the numbering, line 37 is inside the else block (since line 36 `fh = open` is inside else at 12-space indent, and 37 has same indent). Hmm, but that means the stdin path raises NameError at line 40. Unless... the real code differs.\n\nLet me check the actual pytutils source. I'm fairly confident about sarugaku/pytutils/files.py. Let me recall... Actually I can check via get_info! The tool returns info about symbols \u2014 hopefully including source code. Let me call get_info('islurp') and see.\n\nIf the real code has fh_next inside else, then the stdin test would fail with NameError... but then the \"correct\" behavior per the code is that stdin path is broken? That seems unlikely for a library. Let me check the real source via get_info.\n\nActually, I recall the original function is from \"python-utils\"/\"pytutils\" \u2014 the original gist is from @karan? Hmm. Actually \"islurp\" I've seen in \"pytutils\" which is a fork of \"buckx/pytutils\"? Whatever \u2014 get_info will tell.\n\nLet me now write the get_info calls.\n\nAfter that, I'll finalize the tests. If the stdin path would NameError (fh_next undefined), then... hmm, but the task says \"make sure that each test is correct\". If the code as given has fh_next inside else, then a stdin test would raise NameError \u2014 and the test would need to assert that? That seems wrong. Let me check the real code first.\n\nActually, looking at the real pytutils on GitHub (sarugaku/pytutils, file pytutils/files.py):\n\n```python\nimport errno\nimport io\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport functools\n\nfrom .compat import Path\n\n__all__ = [\"islurp\", \"ispew\", \"LINEMODE\"]\n\n\nLINEMODE = -1\n\n\ndef islurp(filename, mode='r', iter_by=LINEMODE, allow_stdin=True, expanduser=True, expandvars=True):\n    \"\"\"Read [expanded] `filename` and yield each (line | chunk).\n\n    :param str filename: File path\n    :param str mode: Use this mode to open `filename`, ala `r` for text (default), `rb` for binary, etc.\n    :param int iter_by: Iterate by this many bytes at a time. Default is by line.\n    :param bool allow_stdin: If Truthy and filename is `-`, read from `sys.stdin`.\n    :param bool expanduser: If Truthy, expand `~` in `filename`\n    :param bool expandvars: If Truthy, expand env vars in `filename`\n    \"\"\"\n\n    if iter_by == 'LINEMODE':\n        iter_by = LINEMODE\n\n    fh = None\n    try:\n        if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:\n            fh = sys.stdin\n        else:\n            if expanduser:\n                filename = os.path.expanduser(filename)\n            if expandvars:\n                filename = os.path.expandvars(filename)\n\n            fh = open(filename, mode)\n\n        fh_next = fh.readline if iter_by == LINEMODE else functools.partial(fh.read, iter_by)\n\n        while True:\n            buf = fh_next()\n            if buf == '':  # EOF\n                break\n            yield buf\n    finally:\n        if fh and fh != sys.stdin:\n            fh.close()\n```\n\nI'm fairly sure `fh_next` is dedented (outside the else) in the real code \u2014 the extracted snippet's indentation is just mangled by the annotation process. The snippet shows line 37 with 12 spaces but that could be an artifact. Hmm, but I should trust the snippet... The snippet's indentation for line 37 matches line 36 (both 12 spaces). If the real code has it dedented to 8 spaces, the snippet would show 8 spaces. The snippet shows:\n\n```\n        36:             fh = open(filename, mode)\n        37:             fh_next = fh.readline if iter_by == LINEMODE else functools.partial(fh.read, iter_by)\n```\n\nBoth 12 spaces. Hmm. But wait \u2014 if fh_next were inside the else, then with filename='-'/allow_stdin, line 40 `buf = fh_next()` \u2192 NameError: free variable fh_next... Actually local variable referenced before assignment \u2192 UnboundLocalError. That would make the stdin feature completely broken, and the docstring explicitly documents allow_stdin. Also line 45 `fh != sys.stdin` check implies stdin path is meant to work.\n\nLet me verify with get_info \u2014 it should give the actual source. I'll ask for 'islurp'.\n\nIf get_info shows fh_next inside else, I'll write the stdin test to expect UnboundLocalError? That'd be weird. More likely get_info will show the true code. Let me just call.\n\nAlso call get_info('LINEMODE') to get its value, and maybe get_info('pytutils.files.islurp')? The tool takes \"class, function or method name, as in 'f' for function f or 'C.foo' for method foo in class C.\" So 'islurp' and 'LINEMODE'.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nThen finalize. I'll write the test file with all tests. Let me draft it now, pending confirmation:\n\n```python\nimport builtins\nimport io\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom pytutils.files import islurp, LINEMODE\n\n\ndef test_islurp_default_reads_lines_and_closes_file(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    path = tmp_path / \"lines.txt\"\n    path.write_text(\"one\\ntwo\\nthree\\n\")\n\n    real_open = builtins.open\n    opened = []\n\n    def tracking_open(file, mode=\"r\", *args, **kwargs):\n        fh = real_open(file, mode, *args, **kwargs)\n        if os.fspath(file) == os.fspath(path):\n            opened.append(fh)\n        return fh\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(builtins, \"open\", tracking_open)\n\n    gen = islurp(str(path))\n    assert next(gen) =