port islurp, LINEMODE\n```\n\nWait \u2014 need to confirm the import path. The code is \"extracted from pytutils/files.py\". So `from pytutils.files import islurp, LINEMODE`.\n\nTest 1: default line iteration + file closed after.\n```python\ndef test_islurp_lines(tmp_path):\n    p = tmp_path / \"lines.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"one\\ntwo\\nthree\\n\")\n    gen = islurp(str(p))\n    assert next(gen) == \"one\\n\"\n    # exhaust\n    assert list(gen) == [\"two\\n\", \"three\\n\"]\n    # file closed after exhaustion (finally ran)\n    ...\n```\nHmm, checking the file is closed: the generator's `fh` is local; we can't easily access it. But we can verify via behavior: after exhaustion, the file handle is closed \u2014 on Windows, tmp_path cleanup would fail if not closed, but that's not an assertion. Alternative: open the file again for writing to verify it's not locked? On POSIX, that doesn't verify. \n\nBetter: monkeypatch? Or use a wrapper: We can check `p.open('a')` works... not a real check on POSIX.\n\nAlternative approach: patch `open`? Hmm, we could monkeypatch io.open... Actually simpler: we can verify closure by checking that reading again works and, more robustly, use `psutil`? Overkill.\n\nA cleaner way: create a file, get generator, exhaust it, then assert that the generator's gi_frame... too hacky.\n\nAlternative: We can test that the file is closed by using a custom subclass? Not with built-in open.\n\nActually, a simple robust check: after exhausting the generator, attempt `os.rename`? On Windows renaming an open file fails. But tests should be platform-independent.\n\nHmm, maybe simpler: monkeypatch builtins.open to return a MagicMock wrapping real file? That gets complicated with readline behavior.\n\nAlternative: We can just trust the finally block and assert content correctness. But the instruction says \"include assertions that verify any applicable postconditions\". The main postcondition is the yielded content. For the close behavior, we can verify via a mock:\n\n```python\ndef test_islurp_closes_file(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    p = tmp_path / \"f.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"data\\n\")\n    real_open = open\n    opened = []\n    def tracking_open(file, mode='r', *args, **kwargs):\n        fh = real_open(file, mode, *args, **kwargs)\n        opened.append(fh)\n        return fh\n    monkeypatch.setattr(\"builtins.open\", tracking_open)\n    assert list(islurp(str(p))) == [\"data\\n\"]\n    assert len(opened) == 1\n    assert opened[0].closed\n```\n\nThat works and verifies the close postcondition. monkeypatch.setattr(\"builtins.open\", tracking_open) \u2014 but the module uses `open` which resolves to builtins.open at call time, so patching builtins.open works.\n\nWait, but the module might do `from io import open`? The shown code uses plain `open`. In pytutils/files.py, there might be `from .compat import ...`. The extracted code shows `import os, sys, functools` and uses `open`. Patching builtins.open should work.\n\nHmm, but careful: monkeypatch.setattr(\"builtins.open\", ...) \u2014 pytest's monkeypatch supports string target \"builtins.open\". Yes.\n\nTest 2: string 'LINEMODE':\n```python\ndef test_islurp_string_linemode(tmp_path):\n    p = tmp_path / \"s.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"a\\nb\\n\")\n    assert list(islurp(str(p), iter_by='LINEMODE')) == [\"a\\n\", \"b\\n\"]\n```\nCovers lines 23-24.\n\nTest 3: chunked:\n```python\ndef test_islurp_chunks(tmp_path):\n    p = tmp_path / \"c.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"abcdefgh\")\n    assert list(islurp(str(p), iter_by=3)) == [\"abc\", \"def\", \"gh\"]\n```\nCovers the functools.partial branch of line 37 and EOF break.\n\nTest 4: stdin:\n```python\ndef test_islurp_stdin(monkeypatch):\n    fake_stdin = io.StringIO(\"from stdin\\nline2\\n\")\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sys, \"stdin\", fake_stdin)\n    assert list(islurp(\"-\")) == [\"from stdin\\n\", \"line2\\n\"]\n    assert not fake_stdin.closed  # stdin must not be closed\n```\nCovers lines 28-29, 39-43, and line 45 false branch (fh == sys.stdin).\n\nNote: islurp reads sys.stdin at call time (`fh = sys.stdin`), so monkeypatching sys.stdin works. Also line 45 checks `fh != sys.stdin` \u2014 since fh IS sys.stdin (the patched one), it won't close. Good.\n\nTest 5: allow_stdin=False with '-' filename:\n```python\ndef test_islurp_dash_no_stdin(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    dash = tmp_path / \"-\"\n    dash.write_text(\"dash\\n\")\n    monkeypatch.chdir(tmp_path)\n    assert list(islurp(\"-\", allow_stdin=False)) == [\"dash\\n\"]\n```\nCovers line 28 false branch (filename == '-' but allow_stdin falsy \u2192 else). Wait, line 28: `if filename == '-' and allow_stdin:` \u2014 with allow_stdin=False, the `and` short-circuits; branch coverage: the condition is False \u2192 goes to else. Good.\n\nHmm, but does the test runner measure branch coverage? The problem says \"execute all missing lines and branches\". So yes, aim for branch coverage too.\n\nTest 6: expanduser:\n```python\ndef test_islurp_expanduser(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setenv(\"HOME\", str(tmp_path))\n    (tmp_path / \"homefile.txt\").write_text(\"home content\\n\")\n    assert list(islurp(\"~/homefile.txt\")) == [\"home content\\n\"]\n```\nOn Windows, expanduser uses USERPROFILE too, but HOME is respected by os.path.expanduser on Windows if set (yes, expanduser checks HOME first on Windows? Actually on Windows, os.path.expanduser prefers USERPROFILE, then HOMEDRIVE+HOMEPATH, then HOME? Let me recall: ntpath.expanduser: checks 'USERPROFILE', then 'HOMEPATH' (with HOMEDRIVE), then 'HOME'? Actually the order: it tries USERPROFILE, then HOMEPATH, then HOME? Let me recall the code:\n\n```python\ndef expanduser(path):\n    ...\n    if 'USERPROFILE' in os.environ:\n        userhome = os.environ['USERPROFILE']\n    elif not 'HOMEPATH' in os.environ:\n        return path\n    else:\n        ...\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, in newer Pythons, ntpath.expanduser: \n```python\nif 'USERPROFILE' in os.environ:\n    userhome = os.environ['USERPROFILE']\nelif 'HOMEPATH' not in os.environ:\n    return path\nelse:\n    drive = os.environ.get('HOMEDRIVE', '')\n    userhome = join(drive, os.environ['HOMEPATH'])\n```\nSo HOME is not used on Windows. Hmm. To be cross-platform, monkeypatch.setenv both HOME and USERPROFILE:\n\n```python\nmonkeypatch.setenv(\"HOME\", str(tmp_path))\nmonkeypatch.setenv(\"USERPROFILE\", str(tmp_path))\n```\n\nOn POSIX, expanduser uses HOME. On Windows, USERPROFILE. Setting both covers both. Good.\n\nTest 7: expanduser=False:\n```python\ndef test_islurp_no_expanduser(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    # literal '~' directory in cwd\n    tilde_dir = tmp_path / \"~\"\n    tilde_dir.mkdir()\n    (tilde_dir / \"f.txt\").write_text(\"literal tilde\\n\")\n    monkeypatch.chdir(tmp_path)\n    assert list(islurp(\"~/f.txt\", expanduser=False)) == [\"literal tilde\\n\"]\n```\nCovers line 31 false branch (skip 32). Note: with expanduser=False, expandvars still True by default \u2014 '~/f.txt' has no $ vars, fine.\n\nTest 8: expandvars:\n```python\ndef test_islurp_expandvars(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setenv(\"PYTUTILS_TEST_DIR\", str(tmp_path))\n    (tmp_path / \"v.txt\").write_text(\"vars\\n\")\n    assert list(islurp(\"$PYTUTILS_TEST_DIR/v.txt\")) == [\"vars\\n\"]\n```\nCovers line 34.\n\nTest 9: expandvars=False:\n```python\ndef test_islurp_no_expandvars(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    literal = tmp_path / \"$LITERAL_VAR\"\n    literal.mkdir()\n    (literal / \"f.txt\").write_text(\"literal var\\n\")\n    monkeypatch.chdir(tmp_path)\n    assert list(islurp(\"$LITERAL_VAR/f.txt\", expandvars=False)) == [\"literal var\\n\"]\n```\nCovers line 33 false branch (skip 34).\n\nTest 10: missing file \u2192 fh stays None, finally short-circuits:\n```python\ndef test_islurp_missing_file(tmp_path):\n    with pytest.raises(IOError):\n        list(islurp(str(tmp_path / \"nope.txt\")))\n```\nCovers line 45 with fh=None (falsy). IOError == OSError in py3.\n\nTest 11: verify close happens (test