ot our concern.\n\nOne more consideration: the stdin test \u2014 `islurp('-')` with default allow_stdin=True. The generator reads from fake stdin. After exhaustion, `fh != sys.stdin` \u2192 fh IS sys.stdin \u2192 no close. assert not fake_stdin.closed. Good.\n\nBut careful: `monkeypatch.setattr(sys, \"stdin\", fake_stdin)` \u2014 inside islurp, `sys.stdin` is looked up at call time \u2192 gets fake. Good.\n\nAlso for the dash-no-stdin test: filename '-' with allow_stdin=False \u2192 goes to else, expanduser('-') = '-', expandvars('-') = '-', open('-') in cwd (chdir'd to tmp_path). Good. Note: on the `fh != sys.stdin` check \u2014 fh is a real file, sys.stdin is real stdin \u2192 closes. Fine.\n\nNow the tracking_open test: patching builtins.open. But note `islurp` calls `open(filename, mode)` \u2014 with builtins patched, tracking_open is called. Good. But other code (pytest internals) might also call open during the test \u2014 we only assert on our tracked handle. We append every opened handle; the last one is ours... Actually pytest might open files during the test (e.g., capture). To be safe, filter by filename:\n\n```python\ndef tracking_open(file, mode='r', *args, **kwargs):\n    fh = real_open(file, mode, *args, **kwargs)\n    if os.path.abspath(str(file)) == target:\n        opened.append(fh)\n    return fh\n```\n\nHmm, simpler: just record handles whose name matches. Or simply assert `opened[-1].closed`? If pytest opens something after... within the test body, between list(islurp(...)) and the assert, pytest shouldn't call open. But capture management happens around tests. I'll filter by name to be safe:\n\n```python\ndef test_islurp_closes_file(tmp_path, monkeypatch):\n    p = tmp_path / \"f.txt\"\n    p.write_text(\"data\\n\")\n    real_open = builtins.open\n    opened = []\n    def tracking_open(file, mode='r', *args, **kwargs):\n        fh = real_open(file, mode, *args, **kwargs)\n        if os.fspath(file) == os.fspath(p):\n            opened.append(fh